{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond II of Antioch was a nobleman in the Crusader states of the Levant he was born in 1140 and died in 1187 Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem making him a member of the prominent House of Poitiers Raymond's father died when he was only a year old and his mother remarried to Baldwin III of Jerusalem who became his stepfather Raymond's early life was marked by the conflict between his mother and stepfather over the succession to the County of Tripoli Raymond's mother Hodierna wanted her son to inherit the county but Baldwin III had other plans he wanted his own brother to succeed instead This led to a power struggle between Hodierna and Baldwin III which ended in 1152 when Baldwin III recognized Raymond as the rightful heir to the County of Tripoli Raymond's reign as count was marked by his continuous struggles to maintain the county's independence from the expanding Muslim states surrounding it In 1157 Raymond was captured by Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo but was released a year later after paying a large ransom Raymond's reign also saw the rise of the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin who sought to expand his control over the Crusader states Raymond tried to form alliances with other Crusader states to resist Saladin's advances but his efforts were in vain In 1187 Saladin launched a major offensive against the Crusader states and defeated Raymond at the Battle of Hattin Raymond was taken prisoner and later executed by Saladin on March 29 1187 Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant he struggled to maintain the county's independence and resisted the expansion of Muslim states around him despite his efforts he was ultimately unsuccessful and met a tragic end at the hands of Saladin.", "